796: **Project watch.** When a Project has an active Lead workspace, `posse up` starts one detached `posse watch --poll-only` process with the Project's `POSSE_HOME`. It has no Herdr pane, does not open a login shell, and uses a per-Project file lock plus a random token and PID recorded in SQLite to prevent duplicate watches. It retains the configured Herdr session, socket, binary/config and Git SSH settings while dropping Worker-pane context, intent hooks and Git worktree selectors. It reconciles PRs, tears down merged Tasks and admits queued Tasks into free Worker slots on each poll. Polls run two seconds apart; the hidden `posse _watch-pass` writes a request token to `projects/<name>/watch-pass.request`, the next poll starts at once, and the command returns after that poll ends and records the token in `watch-pass.done`. E2E fixtures use it to run a poll on demand instead of waiting for one. Its log lines start with an RFC 3339 time. A bounded foreground `posse watch --poll-only --timeout <ms>` run is a User wait, not a managed child. Supervision only adopts managed pollers with a complete private identity; it verifies the executable and reaps an unbound indefinite poll-only process running inside this Project as leaked compatibility state. When no extension or live Lead Watch owns Notice delivery, it may deliver Notices through the configured Lead path; it never acknowledges them. Process identity includes the executable file identity; a heartbeat is refreshed every five seconds. A live watch that fails to make heartbeat progress for 60 seconds raises one `pr_watch_failing` Notice for that stale episode before repair. A stopped watch with a heartbeat older than 60 seconds raises the same Notice during supervision. Stale-watch episode Notices are User-actionable and are sent through `notification.show` and configured `notify.command`; the runtime token deduplicates an episode, and a later runtime token starts a new episode. The Project Watch checks Lead receiver liveness once per minute. It raises `lead_receiver_missing` after an actionable Notice waits 30 seconds without a receiver, then closes that episode when the receiver returns. An unacknowledged User-actionable Notice raises one `notice_delivery_delayed` Notice after one minute, whether delivery is pending or the Lead has not acknowledged it. Acknowledging all overdue Notices closes that alert episode. A missing process is repaired without treating a reboot as an unhealthy heartbeat episode. Stale watches receive SIGTERM and must exit gracefully; supervision never SIGKILLs a watch during Project maintenance. Project maintenance commands, Herdr events, harness hooks and extensions supervise active Project watches. A Pi or OpenCode handoff ensures the independent Project observer is running before its receiver wait, but does not perform observer maintenance inline; adapters own receipt delivery and re-arming. `posse doctor` reports their health, and `posse watch --repair` repairs one without restarting the Lead or Workers. Supervision never starts a watch for a Project marked down. Legacy labelled Watch tabs in the Project workspace are closed during `posse up` and recovery. A 0.14 label is matched only to its recorded pane; unrelated workspaces stay untouched, and an agent occupying a former Watch pane blocks the upgrade. Watch reconcile and watch failures retain the existing visible `pr_watch_failing` behavior; Watch logs and retries transient store contention on the next poll without a Notice. When a held restore, a deferred recovery, an unavailable Herdr or contention keeps the reconcile pass from completing for two minutes, Watch raises one `reconcile_blocked` Notice naming the cause; a completed pass ends the episode. A queued Task's `wait_reason` names that cause while a Worker slot is free. A foreground command that defers reconciliation because its bounded SQLite budget is exhausted continues from durable state and warns that output may be stale. When the recorded Lead pane has been absent for one hour and no Worker is active, the Watch parks the Project: it stops without changing Tasks, Notices or Decisions. `posse` and `posse list --all` show `parked` and the reason; `posse up --all` skips parked Projects and names them, while `posse up` in the Project folder resumes one. A Watch does not deliver Notice alerts to a missing Herdr workspace. [ADR 0025](adr/0025-hidden-supervised-project-watches.md) amends ADR 0001 and narrows ADR 0005's detached-Watch restriction.
797: 
798: **Watch repair.** From the Project folder, the User can run `posse watch --repair` to restore only its watch, including while its restore is held. It verifies the existing active Lead workspace and one matching process; it updates only watch runtime state and never calls `recoverProject`, changes config, restarts the Lead or starts Workers. Repeating the command is a no-op when the watch is healthy. `posse doctor` reports each active Project's watch PID, executable identity, heartbeat and log path.
799: 
800: **PR/MR watch.** Every Task with a `pr_url` is watched in every state until the forge reports it merged or closed, including `working`, `needs-decision`, `blocked`, `stalled`, `failed` and `lost`. A merged head is trusted when it remains on the Sandbox's Task branch or was recorded by `posse publish` (for a workspace Member: equals its gated head or remains on its Task branch), and a merged request must target the Task/member's frozen branch. A merged source head that fails this check, for example after history was rewritten outside `posse publish`, leaves the Task in its current state and raises one `pr_merged_ungated` Notice per Task, Member and merged head instead of a `pr_watch_failing` streak. The Notice names the gated head, the merged head, whether their trees match and the recovery step; once the merged head is on the Task branch, the next poll settles the merge. With `defaults.require_current_target`, open requests are revalidated against that target before recording CI; a target advance during the forge query marks checks `STALE` and cannot emit `merge_ready`. Full-pipeline PRs also use the target's FIFO merge queue when target movement invalidates their candidate, regardless of this setting. The queue orders by each Task's latest done Signal; only the head is asked to refresh and rerun validation. Remaining PRs retain their last green check observation until promoted and are not told that their target moved. A newly promoted head refreshes against the latest target before it can be gated for merge. A `merge_ready` Notice is emitted only while the Task is `done` or `merging`, once per Member head and completion transition, and only when the selected review evidence passes for that head. A policy refusal, such as missing evidence, withholds it; an operational error while evaluating the evidence, such as a store or forge failure, leaves the observation unrecorded so the next poll retries, and counts toward `pr_watch_failing` with its cause. For a done or merging Task, Posse queues the Worker once per completion to merge the target and republish; workspace instructions name the affected Member. An invalid PR URL that points at an issue raises one `pr_closed` Decision to replace the URL or discard the Task. Other persistent PR failures produce a deduplicated `pr_watch_failing` Notice after three consecutive failures at the affected scope, name the reason and retry on the next tick. A failed observation never silently drops the Task. The forge is selected from the request's repository. Watch and doctor use a two-second forge-auth probe timeout; Watch timeouts are not cached, while foreground timeout results are negatively cached for five seconds. Host-level forge-discovery failures and missing GraphQL repository data are recorded once at Project scope; request-specific failures are recorded with the affected Task and Member. Watch polls each Member independently with a durable claim and at most one active forge query per Member. A stalled Member query or fetch does not delay healthy Members' next poll cycle. Watch runs checkout sync and merged-Task Teardown alongside PR polling; a newly merged Task triggers Teardown independently of the checkout join, and a phase failure does not suppress the others. GitHub and GitLab adapters read request state, merge commit, head SHA, mergeability, reviews or approvals, and required checks from each Member's forge. Reconcile polls when the Project's last poll is older than `defaults.pr_poll` (default `2m`); `posse watch` polls at that interval. Pollers atomically claim the Project and each workspace Member's poll in SQLite; duplicate pollers skip active claims, and abandoned claims expire. Each observed change is recorded in `pr_observations` and produces at most one Notice per change:
